    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fracture patterns within the Navajo Formation along the East Kaibab monocline have been studied for the purpose of understanding the mechanisms which govern their formation, orientations, and spatial distributions. The East Kaibab monocline is a strongly asymmetrical fold which, in the study area, accommodates approximately 1500 meters of structural relief between the Kaibab Uplift on the west and the Kaiparowits Basin to the east. Mechanisms of fracturing encountered in the study area include bedding plane slip, bending, nucleation along preexisting deformation bands, and localization near faults and preexisting fractures which have been subjected to later shearing. Fracture set orientations within the Navajo Formation display variations in orientation of up to 30 degrees within relatively short distances (<1km) along strike of the structure where no obvious change in fold strike or dip is detectable. Detailed mapping of the intersection zone between two joint sets demonstrates the influence of preexisting structures on nucleation and growth of new joint sets as the stress field changes during fold development. Preliminary results from this study indicate that, as opposed to traditional fold-related fracturing theories which predict simple geometric relationships between bedding attitude and fracture orientations, even relatively simple fold structures exhibit highly variable joint sets which form by differing mechanisms and at different stages in the growth of the fold.\r\n\r\nFuture work will attempt to refine understanding of the fracturing mechanisms displayed at the East Kaibab monocline and extend the study to other folds which exhibit other stages of fold development. It is hoped that this information will eventually improve our capability to make predictions in similar structures within the subsurface.",
